<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · c. 1859 · Islamic Caliphates &amp; Middle East</p><h1 class="sg-title">Mer Hayrenik (Our Fatherland)</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="hy">Mer Hayrenik</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>The national anthem of Armenia, made from an 1859 poem. It was banned in Soviet times and became the anthem again in 1991.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Mikayel Nalbandian</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Parsegh Ganatchian</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Armenians; the First Republic of Armenia and the Republic of Armenia</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Armenian</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>poem written 1859, published 1861; music early 20th century; anthem 1918, again 1991</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words from a poem by Mikayel Nalbandian (written 1859, published 1861); music by Parsegh Ganatchian (early 20th century))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;Mer Hayrenik&quot; (&quot;Our Fatherland&quot;) is the national anthem of Armenia. Its words come from &quot;The Song of an Italian Girl&quot;, a poem Mikayel Nalbandian wrote in 1859 and published in 1861; the anthem uses the first, third, fourth and sixth stanzas of the poem. The music was composed in the early 20th century by Parsegh Ganatchian.</p><p>The words, with changes, and the music were adopted as the anthem of the First Republic of Armenia, which existed from 1918 to 1920. In November 1920 the Red Army invaded Armenia, only three months after the signing of the Treaty of Sèvres, which had recognised the country as a sovereign state. In 1922 Armenia was absorbed, with Azerbaijan and Georgia, into the Transcaucasian Socialist Federative Soviet Republic, which became part of the Soviet Union at the end of the same year. The Bolshevik authorities outlawed &quot;Our Fatherland&quot; as a symbol of Armenian nationalism; from 1944 the Anthem of the Armenian Soviet Socialist Republic was used in its place. In these years the banned song became a song of protest against Soviet rule.</p><p>After the dissolution of the Soviet Union, the Supreme Soviet of the republic reinstated it as Armenia's national anthem on 1 July 1991, with several words changed from the 1918 version. It is one of the national symbols displayed in Armenian classrooms, and teachers encourage pupils to sing it every day.</p><p>Its words, which praise dying for Armenia's freedom, have been criticised by some commentators as &quot;wimpy&quot; and &quot;gloomy&quot;, and some members of the National Assembly have argued that a song written during the first struggle for independence does not reflect the present. The anthem was debated in the Armenian Parliament in 2006 and in 2019, when proposals were made to bring back the anthem of the Armenian SSR with new words. None of the proposals succeeded; the Armenian Revolutionary Federation (Dashnaktsutyun), part of the governing coalition, resisted them. In 2025 the law on the anthem changed the order of its stanzas: the second became the third, the third the fourth and the fourth the second.</p><p>The anthem has been arranged and adapted. The Italian violist Marco Misciagna composed an &quot;Introduction and Variations on Mer Hayrenik&quot; for solo viola and played it at the Komitas Chamber Music House in 2018, and Philip Sheppard recorded it with the London Philharmonic Orchestra for the 2012 Summer Olympics and Paralympics. A television channel for Armenians living in Russia, based in Novosibirsk, takes its name from the anthem.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A patriotic hymn that calls on Armenians to defend their country's freedom and honours those ready to die for it. Its tone is solemn and earnest; critics have found it more mournful than triumphant.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Armenian</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Armenian</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hy">Մեր Հայրենիք, ազատ անկախ,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hy-Latn">Mer Hayrenikʿ, azat ankax,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Our Fatherland, free, independent,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hy">Որ ապրել է դարեդար,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hy-Latn">Or aprel ē daredar,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>That has lived from age to age,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hy">Յուր որդիքը արդ կանչում են</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hy-Latn">Yur ordikʿə ard kančʿum en</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Its sons are now calling</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hy">Ազատ, անկախ Հայաստան։</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hy-Latn">Azat, ankax Hayastan.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>A free, independent Armenia.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hy">Ահա եղբայր քեզ մի դրոշ,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hy-Latn">Aha ełbayr kʿez mi droš,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Here, brother, is a flag for you,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hy">Որ իմ ձեռքով գործեցի</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hy-Latn">Or im jeṙkʿov gorcecʿi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>That I made with my own hand;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hy">Գիշերները ես քուն չեղա,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hy-Latn">Gišernerə es kʿun čʿeła,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The nights I did not sleep,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hy">Արտասուքով լվացի։</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hy-Latn">Artasukʿov lvacʿi.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>With tears I washed it.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hy">Նայիր նրան երեք գույնով,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hy-Latn">Nayir nran erekʿ guynov,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Look at it, with three colours,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hy">Նվիրական մեկ նշան</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hy-Latn">Nvirakan mek nšan</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>A single sacred sign,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hy">Թող փողփողի թշնամու դեմ,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hy-Latn">Tʿoł pʿołpʿołi tʿšnamu dem,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let it flutter against the enemy,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hy">Թող միշտ պանծա Հայաստան։</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hy-Latn">Tʿoł mišt panca Hayastan.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let Armenia ever be glorious.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hy">Ամենայն տեղ մահը մի է</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hy-Latn">Amenayn teł mahə mi ē</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Everywhere death is the same,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hy">Մարդ մի անգամ պիտ մեռնի,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hy-Latn">Mard mi angam pit meṙni,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>A man must die but once,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hy">Բայց երանի՝ որ յուր ազգի</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hy-Latn">Baycʿ erani, or yur azgi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>But blessed is he who for his nation's</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hy">Ազատության կզոհվի։</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hy-Latn">Azatutʿyan kzohvi.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Freedom is sacrificed.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: After Mikayel Nalbandian's poem (1861); the anthem's text. Text from <a href="https://hy.wikisource.org/wiki/%D5%84%D5%A5%D6%80_%D5%80%D5%A1%D5%B5%D6%80%D5%A5%D5%B6%D5%AB%D6%84" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (Hübschmann–Meillet transliteration).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (4)</summary><ul><li>Romanization: Hübschmann–Meillet, with ու as u, ը as ə, aspirates marked ʿ; the Armenian full stop ։ is written '.' and the ՝ as ','.</li><li>line 1.3: 'Յուր' and 'որդիքը' are the older spellings of 'իր' (its) and 'որդիները' (sons); the line can also be read 'its sons now call out: free, independent Armenia'.</li><li>line 4.1: 'մի է', literally 'is one': death is the same everywhere.</li><li>line 4.4: 'կզոհվի', 'will be sacrificed', i.e. gives his life.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
